# Day 8 — CSS Display & Positioning

## Interview Checkpoint

### 1. What is the difference between `display: none` and `visibility: hidden`?

`display: none` hides the element and removes it from the document layout, so it does not occupy space.

`visibility: hidden` hides the element but keeps its space in the layout.

---

### 2. What is the difference between `display: block` and `display: inline`?

A block-level element normally starts on a new line and can take the available width.

An inline element normally stays on the same line with other inline content and does not behave like a block element for width and height.

---

### 3. What is `display: inline-block`?

`inline-block` allows an element to remain alongside other inline-level content while still allowing control over properties such as width and height.

---

### 4. What is `position: static`?

`static` is the default position value. The element follows the normal document flow.

---

### 5. What is `position: relative`?

`relative` keeps the element in the normal document flow but allows it to be visually moved relative to its original position using properties such as `top`, `right`, `bottom`, and `left`.

---

### 6. What is `position: absolute`?

`absolute` removes the element from the normal document flow and positions it relative to its nearest positioned ancestor.

---

### 7. Why do we commonly use `position: relative` on a parent and `position: absolute` on a child?

We commonly use this pattern when we want to position the child precisely inside the parent.

```css
.parent {
    position: relative;
}

.child {
    position: absolute;
}
```

The child can then be positioned relative to the parent.

---

### 8. What is `position: fixed`?

`fixed` removes the element from the normal document flow and positions it relative to the viewport. It remains in that position while the page is scrolled.

---

### 9. What is `position: sticky`?

`sticky` allows an element to behave normally in the document flow until a specified scroll position is reached, after which it sticks within its scrolling context.

It is commonly used for navigation bars and headers.

---

### 10. Does `position: relative` remove an element from normal document flow?

No.

The element remains in the normal document flow, and its original space is preserved.

---

### 11. Does `position: absolute` remove an element from normal document flow?

Yes.

An absolutely positioned element is removed from the normal document flow.

---

### 12. What does this CSS do?

```css
position: absolute;
top: 0;
right: 0;
```

It positions the element at the top-right of its containing positioning context, usually its nearest positioned ancestor.

---

### 13. What is the difference between `relative` and `absolute`?

`relative` keeps the element in normal document flow and moves it relative to its original position.

`absolute` removes the element from normal document flow and positions it relative to its nearest positioned ancestor.

---

### 14. What is the difference between `fixed` and `sticky`?

`fixed` is positioned relative to the viewport and stays fixed while scrolling.

`sticky` initially behaves as part of the normal document flow and becomes stuck when the specified scroll threshold is reached.

---

### 15. What is the difference between a descendant selector and a child selector?

A descendant selector selects matching elements at any nesting level.

```css
.card p {
    color: blue;
}
```

A child selector selects only direct children.

```css
.card > p {
    color: blue;
}
```

